WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.129 --> 00:00:01.980
(audience clapping)

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.170
- The last time you were
here, what was it to promote?

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.003
Oh, the album!

00:00:05.003 --> 00:00:05.836
- Yes.

00:00:05.836 --> 00:00:06.669
- Okay, now you're touring
and your daughter is

00:00:06.669 --> 00:00:07.502
touring with you.

00:00:07.502 --> 00:00:08.335
How old is she?

00:00:08.335 --> 00:00:10.530
- Yeah, she's two and a half,

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.400
and yeah, she's adorable.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.090
- Probably got her own place
by now living on her own.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.500
They grow up so quickly.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.060
- I know, these past
few years have felt like

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.170
18 years at this point.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.970
So, no, she's adorable.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.070
She, it's, I don't quite know how

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.440
to pull off touring on a bus.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.700
I, she's pretty fearless,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.370
but I think things that
move without her control,

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.110
that's like not her favorite thing.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.520
So I'm hoping she'll like, you know,

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.600
sleep in the bed with me and be okay.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.650
- Is it like, mommy why
does the house keep moving?

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:38.483
- Probably.

00:00:38.483 --> 00:00:39.316
- Do we owe money?

00:00:39.316 --> 00:00:40.149
Why do we keep moving?

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.710
Now, speaking of families,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.870
I heard yours is very competitive.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:46.829
Is that fair to say?

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:47.662
- Yes.

00:00:47.662 --> 00:00:49.080
Uh, yes, I think it's pretty fair.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.940
We play games like Pinochle.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:51.817
I don't know if you've played Pinochle.

00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:54.876
- Pinochle, yeah that's
like a old people game.

00:00:54.876 --> 00:00:56.199
(voices overlap)

00:00:56.199 --> 00:00:57.930
- I'd love to learn, I
think it needs a comeback.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:58.763
That's what that sounds like.

00:00:58.763 --> 00:01:01.260
- We have like, great
grandparents would play

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.850
with grandparents, with parents.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:04.980
So everybody kind of gets
around this one table

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.570
and it gets, you know,
like really intense.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.520
- So great grandparents
play with grandparents,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.353
play with parents,

00:01:09.353 --> 00:01:10.500
so it's all old people.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.150
- Yes, yes.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.970
- There's nobody under 80,
apparently in this game.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:15.803
- Pretty much.

00:01:15.803 --> 00:01:18.000
And there was, I mean, we had like, even a

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.920
like a boxing bell installed

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.510
above the table where you play.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.570
So, as you hit like a
really high bid, "Ding!".

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.183
Or if you win "Ding!".

00:01:26.183 --> 00:01:28.512
- So in case anybody sleeping
that'll wake them up.

00:01:28.512 --> 00:01:29.345
- Yes, yes, yeah.

00:01:29.345 --> 00:01:31.200
No, the things that are screamed

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.790
at the table, no one's sleeping.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.050
- Is that what this video was?

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.280
Is this your Dad ringing the bell?

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.663
Let's take a look.

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:39.840
(bell ringing)

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:40.673
- Adorable.

00:01:42.571 --> 00:01:43.625
That's amazing!

00:01:43.625 --> 00:01:46.683
- Well, he's waiting for the
game to begin apparently.

00:01:46.683 --> 00:01:48.390
Now, you took your family on a trip

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.670
when you were young, that affected you.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:51.503
Tell me about that.

00:01:51.503 --> 00:01:52.350
Where, did you go?

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:55.200
- Uh, yeah we, 'cause I mean,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.520
we've been trying to take Lou

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:57.762
on as many trips as we can.

00:01:57.762 --> 00:01:58.595
- Right.

00:01:58.595 --> 00:02:00.360
- But I remember it meant a lot to me,

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.500
fourth grade,

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.880
we went to Washington DC.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:03.713
- Right.

00:02:03.713 --> 00:02:05.190
- And we saw The Supreme Court.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:06.023
- Right.

00:02:06.023 --> 00:02:08.970
- And I remember just being so awestruck

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.860
that in fourth grade I
decided I would become

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.240
a Supreme Court Justice.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.140
- And how'd that work out?

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:13.973
- I know, right?

00:02:14.823 --> 00:02:17.520
I feel like I just saw
you getting asked this.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.660
- Same, as a Paleontologist.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.360
- And I mean, I think it's
worked out in the sense

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.390
that now I really appreciate that,

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.370
you know, as humans we're not perfect.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.960
As a country, we're not perfect.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.570
But I think we all agree
that we want to be as good

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.760
as we can be, and we're striving for this.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.770
And I think it, you know, it plays out.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:35.940
I work in the music business,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.460
which has some room to grow, I would say.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.140
- And, and two and a half is perfect,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.640
for The Supreme Court, for a child.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.473
- Yeah.

00:02:42.473 --> 00:02:45.180
- Really, all the
excitement, and the glamour,

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.240
and the glitz of The Supreme Court.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.130
- Yeah, right?

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.300
The black robes.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.970
- Oyez is fun, its fun to
hear Oyez all the time, right?

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.083
Yeah, yeah, it gives me
like a, I don't know,

00:02:53.083 --> 00:02:55.487
I get chills still when I
hear like, "Oyez, oyez!".

00:02:55.487 --> 00:02:56.727
You're like "Ahhh!".

00:02:56.727 --> 00:02:57.560
- There you go.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.070
Now, talent show American Anthems.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.320
It also tries to bring out
some truths in America.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.153
Isn't that correct?

00:03:02.153 --> 00:03:03.330
- Yeah. So it's okay, so,

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.820
the concept is that every show

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.670
you get to meet an American Hero,

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.050
and

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.180
us as singer songwriters

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.250
get to meet them and then write a song.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:15.240
They don't know it's happening,

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.073
but we get to meet them,

00:03:16.073 --> 00:03:16.980
understand their story,

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.840
and write a song for them.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:19.673
- Okay.

00:03:19.673 --> 00:03:20.940
- Which is like, as a songwriter

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.790
you're used to digging deep
and finding the emotions.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:24.623
- Right.

00:03:24.623 --> 00:03:26.280
- But to do it so personally, I mean

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:27.330
you're used to this in your job

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.730
looking someone in the
eye and talking to them.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:30.980
- But I make fun of them.

00:03:31.965 --> 00:03:33.840
You meet a hero.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:34.830
- I'm doing a very, you know,

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.680
emotional, heartfelt, I want you to know.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.050
And I mean, this is how I
felt meeting Meredith the fact

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.397
that she, most people see
something and they go,

00:03:42.397 --> 00:03:44.340
"Gosh that could be better.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.660
I'm not going to do anything about it."

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.450
And Meredith said, "I'm
going to put the time

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:51.240
in and change kids' lives
and reach out to people."

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.030
It restored my faith, to
be honest in humanity.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:55.767
'Cause she reached out to
people, anonymous people.

00:03:55.767 --> 00:03:56.600
(audience clapping)

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:57.520
Yeah.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.603
- So what was it she did?

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.150
- So, there's an
organization called Book'em,

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.520
that already collects books

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:06.930
to give to kids that need them.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.940
To schools, organizations,

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.800
and she noticed, because you know,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.870
as a Black woman, and
as a young Black child,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.580
she knows she didn't have, you know

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:16.260
characters that looked
like her in the book.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.620
So she would take brown
crayon and draw herself in.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:19.453
- Right.

00:04:19.453 --> 00:04:21.780
And now as an, as an
adult, she looked at this,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.150
and she's like, you know, my
kids, I want them to have this.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.670
I want these kids that are
getting these books to have this.

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:30.120
So she reached out, just
and kind of put it online

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.777
and to family and friends and said,

00:04:31.777 --> 00:04:33.450
"Do you guys have any books you know,

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:35.760
with Black characters in
them, will you donate?"

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.820
And she just got this
overwhelming response,

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:39.960
of books.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:41.040
And I mean, these are the stories

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.540
that you really want to
be hearing right now.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:43.950
People really do care.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:44.783
- Yeah.

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:45.870
- And she made sure that happened.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.120
And these books are getting
to these kids, and just

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:51.240
meeting her, meeting
the people around her.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.390
The song came out, it was
"Make Tomorrow Come Today".

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:55.680
'Cause I feel like
that's what she's doing.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.257
- Well, great title.

00:04:57.257 --> 00:04:58.090
- Thank you.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:04:59.578
(audience clapping)

00:04:59.578 --> 00:05:00.727
- Well I'm anxious to hear the song.

00:05:00.727 --> 00:05:03.589
Will we hear it this Friday?

00:05:03.589 --> 00:05:05.136
- On the show, yes we will.

00:05:05.136 --> 00:05:06.258
- We'll hear it this Friday, okay.

00:05:06.258 --> 00:05:09.150
American Anthems premieres
this Friday on PBS.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:12.087
Go online now for tickets to
Cam's "The Otherside Tour".

